Use MessageQueueHelpers.pkg

Define CPC_OpenFile          For 1 // perfcounter on open file
Define CPC_BuildCodeExplorer For 2 // perfcounter on the code explorer
Define CPC_ChangeWorkspace   For 3 // perfcounter on changing the workspace

Function _T Global String sText Integer iIndex Returns String
#IFDEF TH_TRANSLATION
    //@ RRS check iIndex against sizeofarray
    If (iIndex < SizeOfArray(gILanguage)) Begin
        Move gILanguage[iIndex] to sText
    End
#ENDIF
    Function_Return sText
End_Function

// Calls the Windows Browse for Folder Window and preselects the passed Directory
// since this can only be done by a callback it must reside in a c dll.
// 28.02.2003 BP
// Disabled for now as we don't supply the parser DLL.
Function SelectFolderWithInit Global String sTitleText String sInitDir Returns String
    Send Info_Box "Oops we are using SelectFolderWithInit"
    Function_Return ""
End_Function

// The three Use lines that used to sit here - cSciCodelist, oEditorProperties and
// THColorer - have moved to the files that actually reference their symbols. This file
// referenced NONE of them; they were bundled at the bottom so that anything reaching
// cSciLexer got the whole editor in one go.
//
// That bundling is what made this library's Use graph CYCLIC:
//   cEditorProperties -> cSciCommandHotKeySupport -> cSciLexerSupport
//                     -> oEditorProperties -> cEditorProperties
// A cycle compiles, but WHICH package enters it first then fixes the compile order of
// everything inside it. So adding a single Use line in a consuming application could
// leave cEditorProperties compiled before the hotkey globals it calls, and every editor
// object then died at Construct with Error 98 on SET_SHORTCUTKEYDEFINITIONS - seen from
// one direction 2026-07-26 and from the other 2026-07-27. With the bundle gone the graph
// is a DAG and entry order stops being load-bearing.
//
// Nothing is lost for existing consumers: cSciLexer.pkg now pulls all three explicitly,
// and that is the file they enter the editor through.
